{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QGeometricLanglands.SpectralHalfLine","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QGeometricLanglands.SpectralHalfLine","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.LocalQZetaFunction","lean:module:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.ReebSpectrum","lean:module:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.WeilZeta","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Analytic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.LogDeriv"],"declarations":24,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_pinned_by_periodicity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_pinned_by_periodicity","kind":"theorem","line":565,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L565-L618","doc":"**The pinning.** A periodic Reeb operator whose spectrum realises the\nFrobenius exponents of a Weil datum has\n`Re ρᵢ = 1/2` *and* `(Im ρᵢ)² = 3/4` — that is, every `ρᵢ` is one of the two\nprimitive sixth roots of unity `1/2 ± i√3/2`.\n\n**This is a sharply falsifiable consequence, and it is the point.** The two\nconstraints are independent in origin — the real part comes from the Weil\nbound, the modulus from the framework's own `reeb_periodic` field — and\ntogether they leave the eight eigenvalues only **two** possible values. A\ngenus-4 curve whose Frobenius eigenvalues are not all\n`q^{1/2 ± i√3/2}` therefore **refutes the conjunction**: either `ξ_q` is not\nthe operator whose spectrum these are, or it is not periodic.\n\nNote this does not contradict Hasse–Weil, which it entails:\n`‖q^{ρᵢ}‖ = q^{Re ρᵢ} = √q` for either value.\n\nA corollary worth stating in words rather than Lean: since\n`1/2 ± i√3/2` are primitive **sixth** roots of unity, `specᵢ^Ω = 1` forces\n`6 ∣ Ω`. So the framework's Reeb period cannot be any natural number — the\nidentification requires it to be a multiple of six.\n\n**WHICH `Ω`, because the glyph is overloaded three ways (bean `qou-9iwt`).**\nThe `Ω` here is the natural number in `op ^ Ω = id` — reading (1) below. It\nis *not*:\n\n* the **topological space** `Ω_q` of `q-plancherel-asymptotic-skeleton`,\n  target of the pushforwards `ι_n : {λ ⊢ n} → Ω_q`; nor\n* the **infinite surreal** `Ω_q ≍ ε⁻¹` of the `appendix-surreals` blocks,\n  with `ε = 1/ω` the Reeb infinitesimal.\n\nA space and an infinite surreal are not natural numbers, so `6 ∣ Ω` says\nnothing whatever about either. Carrying this corollary to them is the\ncategory error AGENTS.md warns of — *\"attributes do not transfer across\noverloaded symbols\"*.\n\n**And the test is not runnable today.** `CategoricalReebDerivation` has\n**no instance** and no `period :=` anywhere in the tree, so reading (1) has\nno value in the corpus and there is nothing for `6` to divide. Inhabiting\nthat structure is the prerequisite. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_norm_one_of_periodic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_norm_one_of_periodic","kind":"theorem","line":558,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L558-L563","doc":"Root-of-unity eigenvalues have modulus one (for a positive period). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_periodic_eigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_periodic_eigenvalue","kind":"theorem","line":538,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L538-L556","doc":"**A periodic Reeb operator has root-of-unity eigenvalues.** If\n`op^Ω = id` then `specᵢ^Ω = 1`.\n\nThis is where `mode_ne_zero` earns its place in the structure: without it the\nzero vector would satisfy the eigenvalue equation for every scalar and\nnothing could be concluded. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_pow_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_pow_apply","kind":"theorem","line":523,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L523-L536","doc":"An eigenvector stays one under iteration: `op^n (modeᵢ) = specᵢ^n • modeᵢ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.eigenvalue_sq_eq_q_of_fixed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.eigenvalue_sq_eq_q_of_fixed","kind":"theorem","line":491,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L491-L503","doc":"The eigenvalue form of the same fact: a fixed index has `αᵢ² = q`. Needs no\nReeb spectrum and no identification — it is a statement about `Z` alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_fixed_point_on_critical_line","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_fixed_point_on_critical_line","kind":"theorem","line":460,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L460-L489","doc":"**A fixed point of the pairing lies ON the critical line — from the\nfunctional equation alone.**\n\nIf the Weil involution fixes index `i`, then `Re ρᵢ = 1/2` exactly, with **no\nCastelnuovo–Severi input**. `reeb_spectrum_on_critical_line` gets the same\nconclusion for *every* `i`, but pays the deep Weil bound for it; this gets it\nfor the fixed indices for free.\n\nWhy that is worth stating separately. `critical_line_iff_fixed` already\nobserves that `Re ρ = 1/2` is *equivalent* to being fixed by `ρ ↦ 1 − ρ`, but\nthat is an identity about real numbers — it holds for any `ρ` whatsoever and\nsays nothing about this spectrum. Here the fixed-ness is supplied by the\nWeil pairing itself, so the critical line stops being a value the spectrum is\nobserved to have and becomes **the fixed locus of a symmetry the functional\nequation forces on it**.\n\nConcretely a fixed index means `αᵢ = q/αᵢ`, i.e. `αᵢ² = q` — the\nsupersingular shape, `αᵢ = ±√q`. `weilZetaExample` is exactly this case at\nevery index, which is why its permutation is `Equiv.refl`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.eigenvalue_functional_equation_involutive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.eigenvalue_functional_equation_involutive","kind":"theorem","line":441,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L441-L458","doc":"**The Weil pairing is an involution on the eigenvalues.** Applying\n`α ↦ q/α` twice returns `αᵢ`.\n\nStated on the eigenvalues rather than on the permutation, because that is\nthe form that is unconditionally true: `σ² = id` as a permutation needs the\n`αᵢ` to be *distinct* (a repeated eigenvalue lets `σ` permute its indices\nwithout moving any value), whereas `α_{σ²i} = αᵢ` holds always. The weaker\nstatement is the honest one and is all the fixed-point result below uses. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_sum_re_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_sum_re_witness","kind":"theorem","line":419,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L419-L439","doc":"**The whole chain is jointly satisfiable, and the trace really is 4.**\n\n`reeb_spectrum_sum_re` is conditional on three things at once — a Weil datum,\na Reeb spectrum, and the identification between them. Each is separately\nknown to be inhabited, which is *not* the same as their being jointly\ninhabited, and a theorem conditional on an unsatisfiable conjunction proves\nnothing.\n\nHere they are met together, on `weilZetaExample` (`q = 4`, every `αᵢ = 2`).\nThat datum is supersingular: `|αᵢ| = 2 = √q`, so every spectral exponent is\n`log 2 / log 4 = 1/2` and the eight of them sum to `4`. The conclusion is\ntherefore exhibited on a concrete case, not merely derived. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_sum_re_of_isWeilZeta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_sum_re_of_isWeilZeta","kind":"theorem","line":408,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L408-L417","doc":"The same two facts packaged for a datum that carries `[IsWeilZeta Z]`, which\nsupplies the permutation. Convenience only — the content is above, and the\nexplicit-`σ` forms are the ones that record which hypothesis was used. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_sum_re","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_sum_re","kind":"theorem","line":369,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L369-L406","doc":"**The Reeb spectrum has trace equal to the genus.** `∑ᵢ Re ρᵢ = 4`.\n\nBring's curve has genus `g = 4`, and `LocalQZetaFunction` carries `2g = 8`\neigenvalues — the `Fin 8` throughout this file. So the statement is\n`∑ Re ρᵢ = g`, and it is forced by the functional equation alone.\n\n**Why this is the useful form.** It follows from\n`reeb_spectrum_on_critical_line` too (eight halves make four), but that route\nneeds Castelnuovo–Severi. Here the hypothesis is only the pairing, and the\nconclusion is a *single real number* computable from any candidate operator\nwithout diagonalising it in the complex plane: the trace of the Reeb\nderivation on the eight fundamental modes, real part, must be `4`.\n\nSo it is a falsification test for `qou-qspc`'s open identification. If the\nframework's `ξ_q` has `∑ᵢ Re ρᵢ ≠ 4` on its eight modes, then no `IsWeilZeta`\ndatum can satisfy `HasReebSpectralCoords` with that operator, and the\nidentification is refuted — without ever touching the deep Weil input.\n\nIt is of course only necessary, not sufficient: `{0.3, 0.7}` sums correctly\nand sits off the line. Scope-(a): passing this test is evidence, not a\nproof. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_reflected_by_functional_equation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_reflected_by_functional_equation","kind":"theorem","line":345,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L345-L367","doc":"**The functional equation reflects the Reeb spectrum in the critical line.**\n\nGiven the Weil pairing `α_{σ i} = q / αᵢ` as a permutation `σ`, the\nidentification transports it to `Re ρ_{σ i} = 1 − Re ρᵢ`. So the multiset of\nreal parts is symmetric about `1/2`.\n\nThis is strictly weaker than `reeb_spectrum_on_critical_line`, and\ndeliberately so: symmetry about the line is not the same as lying on it. A\nspectrum with real parts `{0.3, 0.7}` satisfies this and violates the\ncritical line. Castelnuovo–Severi is what collapses the symmetry onto the\nfixed locus; `critical_line_iff_fixed` names that locus. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.hasReebSpectralCoords_satisfiable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.hasReebSpectralCoords_satisfiable","kind":"theorem","line":297,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L297-L326","doc":"**The identification is a consistent hypothesis.** For every `Z` with `q > 1`\nthere is a `ReebSpectrum` satisfying `HasReebSpectralCoords Z`.\n\nHence `reeb_spectrum_on_critical_line` is not vacuously true: its hypothesis\ncan be met.\n\n**What this does not establish**, and the distinction is the whole point:\nsatisfiability says *some* operator has the Frobenius exponents as its\nspectrum — here a diagonal operator built from them, which is no more than\nbookkeeping. The arc's claim is that the framework's Reeb derivation `ξ_q` is\nsuch an operator. That is a statement about a *particular* operator and is\nuntouched by this. What the result buys is the elimination of one way the arc\ncould have been empty, and a sharper location for the open part: everything\nnow rests on the choice of operator, nothing on the shape of the hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.diagOp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.diagOp","kind":"def","line":291,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L291-L295","doc":"The diagonal operator on `Fin 8 → ℂ` with entries `ρ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_fixed_by_involution","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_fixed_by_involution","kind":"theorem","line":269,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L269-L277","doc":"The same, as the symmetry statement: each Reeb eigenvalue is fixed by the\nfunctional equation's involution on real parts. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_on_critical_line","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.reeb_spectrum_on_critical_line","kind":"theorem","line":236,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L236-L267","doc":"**The Reeb spectrum lies on the critical line.**\n\nThis is what `cor:spectral-half-line` was always meant to say. What it said\nbefore was the same conclusion about unnamed exponents; the `ρ` here are the\neigenvalues of an actual operator, carried by `R`.\n\n**The magnitude is now derived, not assumed.** This statement used to carry\n`(hw : ∀ i, ‖Z.eigenvalues i‖ = Z.q ^ (1/2))` as a hypothesis — the very fact\n`hasse_weil_bound` proves from `IsWeilZeta`, one file away, which this file\ncould not reach because content-block siblings cannot import one another\n(§0a). Lifting the class into `QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.WeilZeta` removed the\nduplication: `[IsWeilZeta Z]` replaces `hw`, and the Hasse–Weil bound is\nimported rather than re-assumed.\n\nSo the result is now conditional on exactly two things, and it is worth being\nprecise about their different standings:\n\n- `[IsWeilZeta Z]` — the two classical Weil inputs (functional equation,\n  Castelnuovo–Severi). Cited results, and `weilZetaExample` witnesses that\n  they are jointly satisfiable.\n- `[HasReebSpectralCoords Z R]` — the identification of the Frobenius\n  exponents with the Reeb spectrum. **This is the open one** (bean\n  `qou-qspc`); no instance exists, and `hasReebSpectralCoords_satisfiable`\n  below shows only that the hypothesis is consistent, not that `ξ_q` meets\n  it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_half_line_is_fixed_locus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_half_line_is_fixed_locus","kind":"theorem","line":212,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L212-L220","doc":"**Assembled.** Under the Hasse–Weil bound, every eigenvalue's spectral\nexponent is fixed by the functional-equation involution — the content of\n`spectral_half_line`, restated as the symmetry statement it is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.critical_line_iff_fixed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.critical_line_iff_fixed","kind":"theorem","line":202,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L202-L210","doc":"**The critical line is the fixed locus.** For `q > 1`, a spectral exponent\nhas real part `1/2` exactly when it is fixed by the functional equation's\ninvolution `ρ ↦ 1 − ρ` on real parts.\n\nSo `Re(ρ) = 1/2` is not a fitted constant: it is the unique vertical line on\nwhich `α ↦ q/α` acts trivially. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.partner_re_eq_one_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.partner_re_eq_one_sub","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L175-L200","doc":"**The pairing acts as `ρ ↦ 1 − ρ` on the spectral coordinate.** If\n`α = q^ρ` and `q/α = q^σ`, then `σ = 1 − ρ` — provided `q > 1`, so that the\nexponent is determined by its value up to the usual period.\n\nStated on real parts, which is where it is unambiguous:\n`Re σ = 1 − Re ρ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_coord_re_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_coord_re_eq","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L143-L155","doc":"The real part produced by the principal branch, in closed form:\n`Re(ρ) = log ‖α‖ / log q`. Combined with `spectral_coord_re_unique` this is\n*the* real part of any spectral coordinate for `α`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_coord_re_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_coord_re_unique","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L126-L141","doc":"**The real part is branch-independent.** `α = q^ρ` pins `ρ` only modulo\n`2πi / log q`, but every such `ρ` has the same real part — so\n`Re(ρᵢ) = 1/2` is a well-posed conclusion despite the map `α ↦ ρ` not being\nwell-defined. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.exists_spectral_coord","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.exists_spectral_coord","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L108-L124","doc":"A spectral coordinate map always exists: on the principal branch,\n`ρ := log α / log q` satisfies `α = q^ρ`.\n\nUses only `q > 1` and `αᵢ ≠ 0`, the latter being a field of\n`LocalQZetaFunction`. This supplies the *coordinate relation* of\n`spectral_half_line`'s `spectral_coord` hypothesis, and nothing more —\nsee the section docstring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_half_line","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_half_line","kind":"theorem","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L57-L87","doc":"**Spectral half-line**: all fundamental Reeb modes lie on the\ncritical line Re(ρ) = 1/2.\n\nGiven the spectral coordinate mapping αᵢ = q^{ρᵢ}, the\nHasse–Weil bound |αᵢ| = q^{1/2} forces Re(ρᵢ) = 1/2.\n\n**Proof**: By `abs_rpow_eq_rpow_re`, |q^{ρᵢ}| = q^{Re(ρᵢ)}.\nCombined with |αᵢ| = q^{1/2}, we get q^{Re(ρᵢ)} = q^{1/2}.\nSince q > 1, the function q^(·) is strictly monotone, giving\nRe(ρᵢ) = 1/2. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:cor:spectral-half-line","label":"cor:spectral-half-line","md":"quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.abs_rpow_eq_rpow_re","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.abs_rpow_eq_rpow_re","kind":"theorem","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L53-L55","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.abs_cpow_ofReal_eq_rpow_re","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.abs_cpow_ofReal_eq_rpow_re","kind":"theorem","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/spectral-half-line.lean#L42-L51","doc":"For real q > 0 and complex ρ, |q^ρ| = q^{Re(ρ)}.\n\nThe proof uses the chain:\n`|(↑q)^ρ| = |exp(ρ · log(↑q))| = exp(Re(ρ · log(↑q)))`.\nFor positive real q, `log(↑q) = log(q) + 0·i`, so\n`Re(ρ · log(↑q)) = Re(ρ) · log(q)` and\n`exp(Re(ρ) · log(q)) = q^{Re(ρ)}` by definition of `rpow`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}